rendered_sha256":"41bbd2b0894c8f5873ed579585e8234440c1fc43c86af56524d348d3101bea42"},"seconds":9.12246561050415,"seed":456567500461671016,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{1}{5}|9+2a| < 1\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Isolate the absolute value expression:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{5}|9+2a| < 1\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides of the inequality by 5 to eliminate the fraction:\n   \\[\n   |9+2a| < 5\n   \\]\n\n2. **Solve the absolute value inequality:**\n   The inequality \\(|9+2a| < 5\\) means that the expression inside the absolute value, \\(9+2a\\), must be between \\(-5\\) and \\(5\\). Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   -5 < 9 + 2a < 5\n   \\]\n\n3. **Isolate the variable \\(a\\):**\n   To isolate \\(a\\), we need to subtract 9 from all parts of the inequality:\n   \\[\n   -5 - 9 < 9 + 2a - 9 < 5 - 9\n   \\]\n   Simplify the inequality:\n   \\[\n   -14 < 2a < -4\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\(a\\):**\n   Divide all parts of the inequality by 2:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-14}{2} < \\frac{2a}{2} < \\frac{-4}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplify the inequality:\n   \\[\n   -7 < a < -2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Express the solution in interval notation:**\n   The solution to the inequality is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{(-7, -2)}\n   \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(-7, -2)}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:899"}
